   "@id": "argument:one-maker",
   "thesis": "one composing person better explains the Socratic–Peripatetic configuration — its divisions, its production mechanics and its editorial multiplication — than separate composing persons do",
   "standing": "argued from the corpus, comparatively; held by the author as biographical identity (2026-11-08 20:00; 2026-11-09 12:05). A biographical hypothesis argued from the corpus and a directly attested biographical identification are different achievements: the first is argued here; the second is unattested for any count (RB).",
   "steps": [
    {
     "step": 1,
     "move": [
      "arg:a1658-nothing-requires-two"
     ],
     "role": "no text requires a second maker"
    },
    {
     "step": 2,
     "move": [
      "arg:a1576-result",
      "arg:a1576-symmetric-null"
     ],
     "role": "two is not established by the passages read as though it were; the discipline's instrument leaves the received relation-type as unestablished as the hypothesis"
    },
    {
     "step": 3,
     "move": [
      "arg:a1576-voices-objects",
      "arg:a1576-capacity-conceded"
     ],
     "role": "one maker can be many voices (Plato) and many objects (Aristotle); the capacity is conceded in the tradition's own testimony"
    },
    {
     "step": 4,
     "move": [
      "arg:a1733-set-explains"
     ],
     "role": "the comparison: the received reading explains each item by a person; the single-maker reading explains the set by one investigation, and the marks"
